5.1 Each layer solves $P_ku=0$ on either side by its source identity. For any such interior restriction define $\langle q^-,gangle=\int_{\Omega^-}(
abla u\cdot\overline{
abla Lg}-k^2u\overline{Lg})$. Differences of lifts are in $H^1_0$, so the weak PDE proves independence; step 1.1 gives $\|q^-\|_{H^{-1/2}}\le C\|u\|_{H^1}$. For the exterior choose a compactly supported lift in a bounded collar and use minus this integral, because the exterior's outward normal on $\Gamma$ is $-n$. This gives $q^+$ with the same bound. They give Green's identity for every interior $H^1$ test and every exterior $H^1$ test supported in a bounded collar and vanishing on its artificial outer boundary. More generally, on a bounded truncation $U=B_R\setminus\overline{\Omega^-}$ with the sphere separated from $\Gamma$, subtract such a compact inner trace lift from a test $v\in H^1(U)$. The remaining test has zero trace on $\Gamma$, and integration by parts near the smooth outer sphere, together with the weak PDE for zero-trace tests, gives $\int_U(
abla u\cdot\overline{
abla v}-k^2u ar v)=\int_{\partial B_R}\partial_ru ar v\,dS-\langle q^+,\gamma^+vangle$ whenever $u$ is smooth near that sphere. Approximation gives the same identity with a weak outer conormal if necessary. No boundary term at infinity is discarded. All these identities use the declared sesquilinear convention. [step 1.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, given, algebra]

## Statement

Take $L=1$, $N=5$, $h=1/5$, and one particle of charge $q=2$ at $X=h/3$. Define the one-dimensional cloud-in-cell (CIC) weights by $w_j(x)=\max\{1-d_L(x,x_j)/h,0\}$, where $d_L(x,x_j)=\min_{k\in\mathbb Z}|x-x_j+kL|$ is the periodic distance. The only nonzero weights at $X$ are $w_0(X)=2/3$ and $w_1(X)=1/3$. The deposited density before neutralization is $(20/3,10/3,0,0,0)$, and subtracting the uniform background $Q/L=2$ gives
$$ \rho=(14/3,4/3,-2,-2,-2),\qquad h\sum_{j=0}^4\rho_j=0. $$ For every grid potential $\phi$, the adjoint identity is $$ \langle\rho,\phi\rangle_h =2\left(\frac23\phi_0+\frac13\phi_1\right)-2\bar\phi. $$

The diagram shows the local CIC stencil between nodes $0$ and $1$; indices on the full grid are understood modulo $5$.

\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,every node/.style={font=\small}]
  \draw[->] (-0.35,0) -- (2.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \foreach \j in {0,1,2} {
    \draw (\j,0.07) -- (\j,-0.07);
    \fill (\j,0) circle (1.4pt);
    \node[below=3pt] at (\j,-0.07) {node \j};
  }
  \fill[red] (0.333,0.55) circle (1.6pt);
  \node[above] at (0.333,0.55) {$X=h/3$};
  \draw[dashed,red] (0.333,0.52) -- (0,0.04);
  \draw[dashed,red] (0.333,0.52) -- (1,0.04);
  \node[red,left] at (-0.05,0.45) {$w_0=2/3$};
  \node[red,right] at (0.95,0.45) {$w_1=1/3$};
  \draw[<->] (0,0.26) -- node[above] {$h$} (1,0.26);
\end{tikzpicture}

